2\\), \\(S = 4\\)\n\n4. **Calculate the number of assignments for each valid combination**:\n   - For \\(C = 5\\), \\(V = 3\\), \\(S = 1\\): We need to choose 5 out of 9 players to choose chocolate, 3 out of the remaining 4 players to choose vanilla, and the last player to choose strawberry. The number of ways to do this is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{9}{5} \\times \\binom{4}{3} \\times \\binom{1}{1} = 126 \\times 4 \\times 1 = 504\n     \\]\n   - For \\(C = 4\\), \\(V = 3\\), \\(S = 2\\): We need to choose 4 out of 9 players to choose chocolate, 3 out of the remaining 5 players to choose vanilla, and 2 out of the remaining 2 players to choose strawberry. The number of ways to do this is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{9}{4} \\times \\binom{5}{3} \\times \\binom{2}{2} = 126 \\times 10 \\times 1 = 1260\n     \\]\n   - For \\(C = 4\\), \\(V = 2\\), \\(S = 3\\): We need to choose 4 out of 9 players to choose chocolate, 2 out of the remaining 5 players to choose vanilla, and 3 out of the remaining 3 players to choose strawberry. The number of ways to do this is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{9}{4} \\times \\binom{5}{2} \\times \\binom{3}{3} = 126 \\times 10 \\times 1 = 1260\n     \\]\n   - For \\(C = 3\\), \\(V = 2\\), \\(S = 4\\): We need to choose 3 out of 9 players to choose chocolate, 2 out of the remaining 6 players to choose vanilla, and 4 out of the remaining 3 players to choose strawberry. The number of ways to do this is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{9}{3} \\times \\binom{6}{2} \\times \\binom{3}{4} = 84 \\times 15 \\times 0 = 0 \\quad (\\text{not valid since we cannot choose 4 players out of 3})\n     \\]\n\n   The valid combinations give us a total of:\n   \\[\n   504 + 1260 + 1260 = 3024\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the remainder when \\(N\\) is divided by 1000**:\n   \\[\n   3024 \\mod 1000 = 24\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\(N\\) is divided by 1000 is \\(\\boxed{24}\\).",
